gitpkg
------

Simple POSIX helper script that grabs the PKGBUILD of Arch Linux packages via
`git`, from either the official Arch repositories or the AUR.

Most packages exist in only one of the two sources, so gitpkg checks both and
clones from the one that has it. When a name exists in both, gitpkg shows the
last git activity of each candidate and asks which source to use:

    librewolf (extra) [Last Git Activity: 2026-09-30]
    librewolf (AUR) [Last Git Activity: 2024-12-01]

This makes it easy to tell which source is actually newer, like when a package
moved from the AUR to the repos. The picker uses `fzf` when it is installed,
and falls back to a numbered prompt otherwise.

dependencies
------------

`git`, `curl`, and `jq` are required. `fzf` is optional.

install
-------

    make install

remove
------

    make remove

usage
-----

    gitpkg [-h|--help]
    gitpkg <package name> <package name>...

license
